\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, N: g8-f6, N: g8-h6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: This is the opening position of the game with White having made the first move c2-c3 (a somewhat unusual opening). The position is still in the early development phase. White's pawn on c3 controls the d4 and b4 squares but doesn't contribute much to rapid development. All my pieces are still on their starting squares, and I need to begin developing while controlling the center.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider several promising opening moves:\n\n1. **e7-e5**: Classical central pawn advance, immediately stakes a claim in the center, opens lines for the bishop on f8 and queen on d8. This is a solid, principled move that fights for central control.\n\n2. **d7-d5**: Another strong central thrust, directly challenging White's control. Opens lines for the c8 bishop and queen.\n\n3. **N: g8-f6**: Develops a knight toward the center, attacks e4 square, and prepares for kingside castling. A flexible developing move.\n\n4. **N: b8-c6**: Develops the queenside knight, but less active than Nf6 in most positions.\n\n5. **c7-c5**: Attacks White's c3 pawn and fights for central space, though it's somewhat committal.\n\nReason: I choose **e7-e5** because it's the most principled response. It:\n- Immediately fights for central control (the e5 and d4 squares)\n- Opens development for my dark-squared bishop (f8) and queen\n- Maintains flexibility in piece development\n- Is consistent with classical opening principles\n- Creates a symmetrical pawn structure that equalizes the position\n\nAgainst White's passive c2-c3, occupying the center with e5 gives Black an excellent position and more active piece play.\n\nAnswer: P: e7-e5"}
