e's knight (if there was one on c3) or develops with tempo, but c3 pawn controls this square making it less effective.\n\nReason: I choose **N: f6-d5** because it places my knight on a powerful central outpost where it cannot easily be driven away by pawns. From d5, the knight controls critical squares, pressures White's position, and follows the principle of occupying central squares. This move is more solid than Ng4 (which can be pushed back) and more immediately challenging than developing the bishop.\n\nAnswer: N: f6-d5"}
