TH CHECK. This forces White to respond immediately, either by moving the King (losing castling rights permanently if Ke1 moves) or blocking with a piece. This gains tempo and disrupts White's coordination.\n\nReason: Developing with check is almost always strong in the opening when the opponent's King is still in the center. The Bishop to b4 gives check, develops a piece actively, and forces White into a defensive response. After White deals with the check, I can continue developing my other pieces while White's King remains unsafe in the center. This maximizes my winning chances by exploiting White's undeveloped and unsafe King position.\n\nAnswer: B: f8-b4"}
