\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|    |    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, Q: d8-e7, Q: d8-f6, Q: d8-g5, Q: d8-h4, Q: d8-e8, P: e5-d4, P: e5-f4, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, R: f8-e8, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, K: g8-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black and analyzing the current position. White has a queen aggressively placed on g2, which is threatening my kingside. My king is on g8 and has moved (castled kingside). White's king is still on e1 and hasn't castled yet. I have a central pawn on e5 which is well-advanced. White has pawns scattered on a4, c3, d4, e4, f3, h4, and a bishop on f4. My pieces are mostly on the back rank with limited development.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most promising moves:\n\n1. **Q: d8-h4** - This attacks White's h4 pawn and puts pressure on White's kingside. The queen would be actively placed, potentially creating threats against White's uncastled king.\n\n2. **Q: d8-g5** - Attacks the f4 bishop and puts the queen on an active square, but the bishop can easily move.\n\n3. **P: e5-d4** - Captures White's central pawn, opening lines and gaining material.\n\n4. **P: e5-f4** - Captures the bishop on f4, winning a piece outright! This would be excellent if the bishop is indeed on f4.\n\n5. **N: b8-c6** - Develops a knight towards the center, but this is slow given the tactical nature of the position.\n\nLooking at the board more carefully, I see White has a bishop on f4 (BWF1). The pawn capture e5xf4 would win this bishop!\n\nReason: The move P: e5-f4 captures White's bishop on f4, winning a minor piece (worth about 3 points of material). This is a concrete material gain that significantly improves my position. Winning a piece without compensation is typically decisive in chess.\n\nAnswer: P: e5-f4"}
